{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query.** `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next/skills` selects the `<root>` of five files: `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `^name:` front matter under `assets` finds exactly those five files and no others. The reference files under `assets/skills/*/references/` have no front matter and are correctly not selected. The query has a match snippet with front matter and a no_match snippet without it, so its snippets show both what it selects and what it excludes.\n\n**Obligations.** The requirement has two: (a) each skill's workflow ends by naming the skill for the next task, or one skill per outcome, and (b) an outcome with no further task needs no skill named. Each site upholds (a) on its own. The sites carry their own reviews, so I did not vet them. Clause (b) is a permission and needs no test.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_workflows_end_by_naming_the_skill_for_the_next_task]` takes the last numbered step of each of the five skills through `workflow_steps`. It uses `assert_text` to require a phrase naming the next skill:\n\n- `habeas`: \"Follow that skill\". Its routing table is checked separately, so the skill really names the others.\n- `habeas-requirements`: \"Next, follow the habeas-evidence skill\".\n- `habeas-evidence`: \"return to the habeas-requirements skill\".\n- `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`: \"follow the habeas-requirements skill\".\n- `habeas-refactor`: both the habeas-evidence and habeas-requirements skills, one for each outcome.\n\nThe test also asserts that `habeas`'s step 1 table contains a `| name |` row for every other skill in `SKILLS`. Together these cover the clause about naming the skill for each outcome.\n\nI checked the actual workflow text against the assertions. Each last step does end with the named skill. In `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` the \"Next:\" sentence sits in the last step, step 9. The `assert_text` assertions check the final step of each skill, so a skill whose last step dropped its next-skill naming would fail.\n\nThe test hard-codes five skills, but `SKILLS` and the site query agree on the same five. I found no gap.",
  "references": "cad77bef8fd9a6e827e81314c255639bcbcc74e3c865da250136997ac253fa61",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.next",
        "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
